{"attack":true,"target_alias":"SRC_001","cited_input_aliases":["SRC_001"],"claim":"The target's mechanism for centralized resilience (global coordinated mitigation via a control plane) is the very architecture that makes correlated control-plane failure most catastrophic, so it does not establish superior resilience to correlated failure.","grounds":"The problem asks to argue whether a decentralized network is more/less resilient to correlated failure than one large, strictly-more-redundant service. The target asserts centralized resilience via a global control plane that can detect and isolate failing versions instantly. But its own countercondition concedes the central control plane can become a SPOF. If the shared control-plane logic or shared dependent library is hit by a correlated trigger, all redundant heterogeneous stacks fall under one coordination authority simultaneously. The target provides no evidence distinguishing truly independent mitigation from apparent independence until the shared control plane fails; thus it assumes away the correlated-failure class the problem specifically demands evidence for.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"A large service runs N-version programming across redundant stacks, all governed by one global control plane for detection and isolation.","shared_trigger":"The control-plane component (or its shared health-check library) has a date-handling bug triggered by a leap second (SRC_001's own example trigger).","outcome":"The coordinator either marks healthy nodes as failed, isolates the wrong versions, or freezes mitigation because its own decision layer is correlated-failing. The redundant heterogeneous stacks remain available, but the global mitigation mechanism actively breaks recovery.","comparison":"A decentralized network, lacking a shared coordination layer, has no single mitigation authority to fail synchronously. Some independent operators may independently apply local fixes, leaving at least parts of the network operational.","implication":"The target's mechanism only shows resilience when the central coordinator is independent of the trigger. It does not meet the problem's evidentiary bar for distinguishing truly independent mitigation from merely-uncorrelated-until-shared-cause failure."}]}